  "problem": "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-5))^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-5$ and $k=3.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,-5,3\\,).$",
